A||N||11||Firefly||Steering Stabilizer - car shakes at  >50||||1||
Z||000000||Firefly||06-25-2001||01:38 PM||||I ordered a Monroe Steering Stablilizer thru Rockauto.com for '84 Fiero<BR>They sent it to me, Monroe part# SC-2943<BR>but its much thicker part (dia)<BR>I can set the part on, but don't dare to tighten the bolts down, it hitting the rack and will bend.<P>they say its the right part, can someone tell me what Part# are for a Monroe and OEM that does fit.<P>oh. the problem with car it shakes when it gets up to 50mph and over. <BR>any other suggestions of causes?<BR>State inspection failed it for dampening/suspension (yes NJ state test for it)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steve Normington||06-25-2001||01:40 PM||||Has the car has a recent alignment/tire balancing? Do you feel the shaking in the steering wheel or through the body of the car?||||reg||1
Z||000002||SteveJ||06-25-2001||01:51 PM||||Your shaking over 50 is unbalanced wheels. Damper is a separate issue...and no don't tighten it, it is the wrong one. Give 'em hell and make 'em send the right one.<p>[This message has been edited by SteveJ (edited 06-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Firefly||06-25-2001||02:00 PM||||I feel the shake thru steering<BR>I do not know the history on car<BR>it was a traded in thru dealer and was wound up at a bodyshop part yard. was still a whole car and drove it home.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fiero_24||06-25-2001||02:46 PM||||Check the bushing inside the boot.  My rack was cracked and it shook the car really bad.  The replacement rack has a Monroe stabalizer on it.  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011499.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011499.html</A> <P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick's Dad||06-26-2001||10:54 AM||||1) As stated above, check wheel balance.<P>2) As stated before, check alignment. Also check tires for cupping (Cup shaped "cutouts," typically along inside of tread.) These may not be related, as tires can easily be switched from car to car. Prolonged use, however, will knock you car out of alignment if it was originally in.<P>3) Check ball joints. These will grow progressively worse, until a wheel and related components fold themselves into the wheel well. Not good at 50MPH.<P>4) Check steering linkage. This will be the least likely, and will manifest itself at any speed.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||06-26-2001||01:13 PM||||Just leave the steering damper off.<BR>I did a few months ago and haven't missed it since.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Firefly||06-26-2001||02:49 PM||||I found no play in the wheel (wheel off ground) left to right rock or toe rock.<BR>I yet have to try a set of wheels from another fiero.<P>leave dampener off? I don't think so... that wouldn't solve the shake problem.||||reg||4
Z||000008||SteveJ||06-26-2001||03:11 PM||||The damper has nothing to do with the shaking. It doesn't cause it and it won't cure it. It is useful to absorbe bumps encountered on the road which otherwise would knock the steering assembly unpleasantly (bumpsteer). That's why some people take it off. They don't care if the steering wheel jumps a bit. They want the road feel and steering cripness of not having it there. If NJ says it has to be there then you gotta.<P>Take a quick look at the rack to insure it isn't broken as described above then follow the list by Patrick's Dad doing the alignment last as all the other have to be good to get a good alignment. I bet that solves it.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by SteveJ (edited 06-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||06-26-2001||03:15 PM||||I took my dampener off when I started building my car. never have missed it. No shaking at all. Like said above, its either wheels out of balance, bent rim, bad tire. You can try it by moving the front tires to rear and trying it if there close to same size. (assuming rear ones are ok)||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||06-26-2001||04:51 PM||||I know that the damper doesn't affect the shaking. Worn out bushings can also cause the wheel to shake.<P>Kickback and bumpsteer are two different things. The damper controls kickback, but has little to no effect on bumpsteer.||||reg||1
Z||000011||DavidHenshaw||06-26-2001||06:58 PM||||I had the same problem, shaking steering wheel above 50mph. <P>Replaced steering damper - no inprovement<BR>Replaced front shocks - no improvement<BR>Put two new tires on front - problem magically gone and ooh is the road smooth again!!<P>Dave<P>86 GT<BR>||||reg||1
